**A — dirty flag (recommended).** Add a `lintDirty` boolean. When a request is dropped because one is in flight, set it; when the in-flight one finishes, clear it and re-run once.

Smallest diff, and it matches the existing idiom — the class already coordinates with `lintRequested` and `renderRequested` booleans. One extra pass per completed pass, so no unbounded recursion: the flag is boolean, so a burst of 17 keystrokes collapses into exactly one follow-up lint.

**B — single-flight promise chaining.** Keep the in-flight promise in a field and chain the next request onto it. More faithful to "queue", but it restructures the method and introduces promise-lifetime questions the current code doesn't have. More diff for the same observable behaviour.

**C — extract a proper scheduler/debouncer.** Cleanest long term, wrong for this PR — it's a refactor wearing a bugfix's clothes, and the maintainer's policy explicitly asks for small, single-purpose patches.

I recommend A.

## Design

**The change**, in `packages/lint-framework/src/lint/LintFramework.ts`:

```ts
private lintDirty = false;
```

In `requestLintUpdate`, the branch that currently falls through and does nothing gains an else:

```ts
if ((!this.lintRequested && this.targets.size !== 0) || immediate) {
    if (!immediate) this.lintRequested = true;
    // ... existing lint + render ...
    if (!immediate) this.lintRequested = false;
    this.requestRender();

    if (this.lintDirty && !immediate) {
        this.lintDirty = false;
        void this.requestLintUpdate();
    }
} else if (!immediate && this.lintRequested) {
    this.lintDirty = true;
}
```

The `this.lintRequested` guard on the else matters: we only want to mark dirty when the reason for dropping was an in-flight lint. If it was dropped because `targets.size === 0` there's nothing to re-run, and `addTarget` already calls `update()`.

**What I'm deliberately not touching:** the 1000 ms safety-net timer stays (it covers editors that don't emit events — different problem), the debounce branch stays, and `retries`/`repeatEach`/`trace` in the Playwright config stay. Those are yours or a later PR's.

**One adjacent bug I want your call on.** The `await this.lintProvider(...)` has no `try`/`finally`, so if it ever rejects, `lintRequested` stays `true` forever and the framework stops linting permanently. That's pre-existing, but it's the same failure mode the fix is about, and the dirty flag inherits it — a rejected lint would also strand `lintDirty`. Wrapping the body in `try`/`finally` is about three lines.

Include it, or leave it for a separate PR? I lean include — the fix isn't robust without it — but it does widen the diff beyond the one-line story.

**Verification:** `just format`, `just check-js`, and a local `just test-firefox-plugin` run if you want the 27 minutes spent; then draft PR and watch CI.

