New private field `lintDirty = false`. The branch that currently falls through
gains an else; the body is wrapped so the mutex is always released; and the
re-run happens after that release:

```ts
if ((!this.lintRequested && this.targets.size !== 0) || immediate) {
    if (!immediate) {
        this.lintRequested = true;
        // This pass reads the current text, so it satisfies everything
        // requested up to now. Only input arriving during the await counts.
        this.lintDirty = false;
    }

    try {
        // ... existing lint work ...
        this.lastLints = /* ... */;
        this.requestRender();
    } finally {
        if (!immediate) this.lintRequested = false;
    }

    if (!immediate && this.lintDirty) {
        this.lintDirty = false;
        void this.requestLintUpdate();
    }
} else if (!immediate && this.lintRequested) {
    this.lintDirty = true;
}
```

Three ordering constraints, all load-bearing:

- **The re-run must sit after the `finally`, not inside the `try`.** If it ran
  while `lintRequested` were still `true`, the recursive call would hit the
  same guard, set `lintDirty` again, and be dropped — reproducing the exact bug
  this fixes, just one level deeper.
- **`lintDirty` is cleared when the pass starts, not only when it re-runs.** A
  pass reads the text as it is at that moment, so it already covers every
  request made before it began. Clearing at the start means the flag means
  precisely "input arrived while this pass was in flight", which is the only
  case needing a follow-up.
- **The else only marks dirty when `lintRequested` is true.** A drop caused by
  `targets.size === 0` has nothing to re-run, and `addTarget` already calls
  `update()`.

`requestRender()` stays inside the `try`, preserving today's behaviour that a
failed lint does not re-render.

The `try`/`finally` also fixes a pre-existing bug: `lintProvider` rejecting
currently strands `lintRequested` at `true` forever, permanently stopping all
linting. The dirty flag would have inherited that.

On rejection the exception still propagates and the re-run is skipped, leaving
`lintDirty` set. That is harmless — the next call does a fresh lint regardless,
and at worst causes one redundant follow-up pass.

